WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.740
For more than two decades, COPS has provided
American families with something to watch

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:13.170
on Saturday nights. It's also opened a window
into the work of various local law enforcement

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:18.110
agencies. The craziness we see on the air
is just what goes on in front of the camera,

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:22.430
so here are just a few things that have gone
on behind the scenes.

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.100
COPS exists because of a writers' strike

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.730
In researching his 1983 film Cocaine Blues,
COPS co-creator John Langley went on ride

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:36.969
alongs to a couple of drug busts. His experience
gave him the idea for COPS. Langley also worked

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:42.719
on the Geraldo Rivera-hosted, poorly-received
American Vice: The Doping of a Nation. Despite

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:47.559
the unfortunate association with Geraldo,
Langley was able to come up with a winning

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:52.739
idea. At the same time, Hollywood was suffering
through a Writers Guild of America strike,

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:58.690
and networks had a need for unscripted programming.
This perfect storm birthed COPS, and American

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.829
television has never been the same.

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:04.800
Some cities refuse to allow filming

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.689
Because police officers conduct serious work,
COPS' film crews can't randomly show up at

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:14.330
any precinct and just start filming. After
all, having a camera crew following you around

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:19.330
all night can be annoying, as well as dangerous.
Langley fought through this initial reluctance

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.630
in order to get his show on the air:

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:26.030
"Originally, when we started COPS, nobody
wanted to be on COPS, including the cops!

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.040
I mean, it was a very bold idea."

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.320
The film crews need to get permission, and
not all precincts comply. Both Honolulu and

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.369
Chicago have refused to allow filming in their
cities, even after multiple requests. The

00:01:39.369 --> 00:01:44.409
reason for the refusals is that police work
is not entertainment, and COPS "trivializes

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:47.680
policing," according to the Chicago Police
Department.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.490
Producers have been accused of breaking the
rules

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:55.390
COPS made an entire episode out of the arrest
of Dalia Dippolito, who was caught trying

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:00.520
to hire a hit man to murder her husband. The
TV series was filming in the area when the

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:05.170
local PD’s sting operation went down, and
COPS got Dippolito’s signature on a release

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.399
form so they could air her arrest. Dippolito
claims the release form was misrepresented

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:14.690
to her, which the police did not deny. The
little lie didn't affect the jury, who still

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:19.290
found her guilty. She was originally sentenced
to twenty years in prison, but her conviction

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:25.770
was reversed in 2014. Dippolito is currently
awaiting retrial. Maybe COPS can film a sequel

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.360
episode!

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.860
Police accidentally killed a crew member

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.819
With all the danger of filming COPS, it’s
not completely surprising that someone has

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:39.250
been killed during production. In August of
2014, police accidentally shot and killed

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:44.629
sound engineer Bryce Dion. The incident began
when a pellet gun-wielding suspect pointed

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:50.120
his weapon at a Wendy's employee, and the
police opened fire. Police killed the suspect,

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:55.000
along with Dion, who was hit by friendly fire.
While police claim the suspect opened fire

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.090
on them, the suspect's family claim the officers
were just excited to be filmed, and the tragic

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.620
incident resulted from them showing off for
the cameras.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.440
Burt Lancaster was involved

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.870
Before the folks at Fox felt COPS was just
about ready to go, they decided it needed

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:16.739
one more thing: Star power. So in 1989, who
was the biggest star they could get? Tom Cruise?

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:23.069
Michael Keaton? Patrick Swayze? Nope. Instead,
Golden Age-Hollywood handsome guy Burt Lancaster

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:28.159
loaned his vocal talents to the show. The
From Here to Eternity star delivered the opening

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:32.290
disclaimer for the 1989 pilot episode, and
that was it:

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:37.440
"COPS is about real people, and real crime.
It was filmed entirely on location, with the

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.810
men and the women who work in law enforcement."

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.830
There's no word on how much it cost to hire
Lancaster, who hadn't had a major role in

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:49.510
almost a decade. But seeing as how they used
his voice just once, let's hope for Fox's

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.930
sake it wasn't a lot of money.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.049
It triumphed over cancellation

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:59.680
After over two decades as one of Fox's go-to
hits, the network pulled the plug on COPS.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:05.400
But fans of the boys in blue didn't have to
worry. Spike TV soon picked up the show. Now,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.739
COPS can be viewed alongside such dynamic
fare as Ink Master, Bellator MMA, and Lip

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:11.439
Sync Battle.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:20.500
(dramatic music playing)

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.980
Things have never looked better for American
law enforcement officers. And heck, America.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.139
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:33.320
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know what you think of COPS...

